kitty upstream fixes 7e88f51ed7 and 0ea80af638, backported — applies to v0.48.2 (2cb1d95c3a) with
`git apply`, after kitty-upstream-defects.patch, kitty-talk-thread-survives-v0.48.2.patch and
kitty-fullscreen-destroy-v0.48.2.patch (its context includes that patch's code, so it applies only
after it).
(git apply ignores every line above the first diff header, so this block is commentary only.)

Why (2026-10-16, after upstream review of our crash reports): the maintainer answered
kovidgoyal/kitty#10671 (the fullscreen-close SIGSEGV of 2026-10-15) with 7e88f51ed7 and #10672
(`@ action --match` ignoring its match) with 0ea80af638. Neither is in a kitty release yet (latest
v0.49.2, 2026-10-01), and upstream declined a deferred close, so our fullscreen-destroy patch stays
and this one carries upstream's fixes on top of it. Upstream's hunks do not apply to 0.48.2's brace
style, so they were rebased by hand onto v0.48.2 plus the three patches before this one.
Evidence: docs/research/kitty-crash-2026-10-15/README.md.

From 7e88f51ed7, glfw/cocoa_window.m, three of its four hunks:
- hunk 1, -[GLFWWindow removeGLFWWindow]: remove only the screen-parameters observation GLFWWindow
  added, not every observation with the window as observer (our deferred close runs this before the
  exit transition, so the blanket removeObserver: also dropped Cocoa's own).
- hunk 3, -[GLFWWindow toggleFullScreen:]: skip update_titlebar_button_visibility_after_fullscreen_transition
  when glfw_window is NULL, a NULL dereference reachable during our deferred close.
- hunk 4, _glfwPlatformDestroyWindow: skip the Tahoe zero-size frame for a window still in
  fullscreen (here, traditional fullscreen, the one case our patch still closes in place).
- hunk 2 (canBecomeMainWindow NULL check) is DROPPED: kitty-fullscreen-destroy-v0.48.2.patch already
  adds the same `if (!glfw_window) return NO;`, and applying upstream's hunk by fuzz duplicates it.

From 0ea80af638, kitty/rc/action.py and kitty/rc/base.py: windows_for_match_payload takes the payload
key to read, and `@ action` passes 'match_window', the key its client sends. This replaces Part 2 of
kitty-fullscreen-destroy-v0.48.2.patch, removed the same day. Its docs/changelog.rst hunk is dropped.

Build marker (local, not upstream): an empty ObjC class GLFWFsFixV2 in glfw/cocoa_window.m. It exists
only so scripts/kitty-build-swap.sh (`has_fsfix`, a grep of glfw-cocoa.so) and the fullscreen guard's
kitten (objc_getClass in the running kitty) can tell a build carrying this backport from the earlier
build that has only GLFWDeferredClose; `arm` refuses a staged bundle without it.

Built by: scripts/kitty-build-swap.sh build --no-band (applies the four patches in order; this is
the fourth).

diff --git a/glfw/cocoa_window.m b/glfw/cocoa_window.m
index db39155..6be5779 100644
--- a/glfw/cocoa_window.m
+++ b/glfw/cocoa_window.m
@@ -2272,7 +2272,10 @@ - (void)screenParametersDidChange:(NSNotification *)notification {
 - (void) removeGLFWWindow
 {
     glfw_window = NULL;
-    [[NSNotificationCenter defaultCenter] removeObserver:self];
+    // Remove only our own observation, a blanket removeObserver: would also
+    // remove any observations Cocoa has registered with this window as the
+    // observer.
+    [[NSNotificationCenter defaultCenter] removeObserver:self name:NSApplicationDidChangeScreenParametersNotification object:nil];
 }
 
 - (BOOL)validateMenuItem:(NSMenuItem *)item {
@@ -2358,7 +2361,7 @@ - (void)toggleFullScreen:(nullable id)sender
     // When the window decoration is hidden, toggling fullscreen causes the style mask to be changed,
     // and causes the first responder to be cleared.
     if (glfw_window && !glfw_window->decorated && glfw_window->ns.view) [self makeFirstResponder:glfw_window->ns.view];
-    update_titlebar_button_visibility_after_fullscreen_transition(glfw_window, false, !is_fullscreen_already);
+    if (glfw_window) update_titlebar_button_visibility_after_fullscreen_transition(glfw_window, false, !is_fullscreen_already);
 }
 
 // Used only by GLFWDeferredClose after the GLFW window is gone, when toggleFullScreen: above
@@ -2635,6 +2638,14 @@ - (void)windowDidFailToExitFullScreen:(NSWindow *)w { (void)w; [self after:0 run
 - (void)windowWillClose:(NSNotification *)notification { (void)notification; closed = true; }
 @end
 
+// Build marker, no behavior. It exists only so that scripts/kitty-build-swap.sh (a grep of
+// glfw-cocoa.so) and the fullscreen guard's kitten (objc_getClass in the running kitty) can tell a
+// build carrying the upstream backport from one that has only GLFWDeferredClose.
+@interface GLFWFsFixV2 : NSObject
+@end
+@implementation GLFWFsFixV2
+@end
+
 void _glfwPlatformDestroyWindow(_GLFWwindow* window)
 {
     GLFWWindow *w = window->ns.object;
@@ -2681,10 +2692,15 @@ void _glfwPlatformDestroyWindow(_GLFWwindow* window)
     // Workaround for macOS Tahoe where if the frame is not set to zero size
     // even after NSWindow::close the window remains on screen as an invisible
     // rectangle that intercepts mouse events and takes up space in mission
-    // control. Sigh.
-    NSRect frame = w.frame; frame.size.width = 0; frame.size.height = 0;
-    [w setFrame:frame display:NO];
-    [w setHasShadow:NO];
+    // control. Sigh. Not done for full screen windows as there -close starts
+    // the exit full screen transition which restores the pre-full screen frame
+    // anyway, and feeding a degenerate frame to that transition is asking for
+    // trouble.
+    if (!([w styleMask] & NSWindowStyleMaskFullScreen)) {
+        NSRect frame = w.frame; frame.size.width = 0; frame.size.height = 0;
+        [w setFrame:frame display:NO];
+        [w setHasShadow:NO];
+    }
     [w close];  // sends a release to NSWindow so we dont release it
     window->ns.object = nil;
 }
